 {"clause_id": "c001", "issues": [
   {"issue": "This clause fixes the integrated documents as \"Parts I-VI ... as well as the Rate Sheet(s), Personal Fee Schedule(s) and Account Maintenance Information grid(s)\", but Part VI's \"Entire Agreement; Conflict Of Terms\" clause declares the Night Depository Agreement to be the entire agreement on that service and to control over the Deposit Account Agreement. Which document integrates which, and whether items such as the \"Important Information for Consumers about your TD Bank Checking Account\" brochure referenced in the Overdrafts section are part of the contract, cannot be settled from this clause alone.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c002", "issues": [
   {"issue": "\"Account\" is defined here as only Checking, Money Market, personal CD and/or Savings Accounts, while Part II §1 (Accounts Covered) sweeps in \"ALL personal deposit Accounts\" including Passbook Savings, and other provisions govern Health Savings, Holiday Club and Club Saver Accounts. Whether obligations framed in terms of \"Account\" reach those other account types is unresolvable from this definition.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.29}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c003", "issues": [
   {"issue": "\"Business Day\" is defined as every day except Saturdays, Sundays and federal holidays, but the Funds Availability Policy repeatedly qualifies it as \"a Business Day that we are open\" and \"the next Business Day we are open\", implying Business Days on which the Bank is not open. The two usages cannot both be given the defined meaning.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.17}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c004", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c005", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c006", "issues": [
   {"issue": "\"You\" is defined as each depositor and any joint owner, but Part VI (Night Depository Agreement) separately provides that \"you\" and \"your\" refer to \"the Depositor\", and other provisions address custodians, trustees and Authorized Signers who are not depositors or joint owners. Which persons are bound by obligations addressed to \"you\" is therefore contestable.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.13}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c007", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c008", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The clause makes the Agreement control wherever its provisions \"vary from\" the UCC as adopted where the Account was opened, without identifying which UCC provisions are variable; UCC Article 4 makes some duties (good faith, ordinary care) non-disclaimable. The word \"vary\" and the unlimited scope of the override are what a court would have to construe.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.38}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c009", "issues": [
   {"issue": "\"Any additional reasonable charges we may impose for services you request which are not contemplated by this Agreement\" leaves \"reasonable\" undefined and gives no benchmark, while simultaneously allowing the Fee Schedule to be \"amended from time to time\". Whether a given charge is \"reasonable\" or was \"requested\" is left open.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.29}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c010", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The prohibition extends to restricted transactions processed through \"your Account or any relationship between you and the Bank\", a phrase with no stated boundary. What counts as a \"relationship\" outside the Account is undefined.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.10}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c011", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c012", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The consent is triggered only \"if you give a cell phone number directly to us\", leaving unresolved whether numbers supplied to agents, on applications, or later acquired are covered, and it is open-ended as to duration and revocability. \"Directly to us\" is the term a court would construe.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.21}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c013", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The clause makes the \"form of ownership specified on the signature card\" decisive and says \"only the portion corresponding\" applies, but supplies no rule where the card is silent, ambiguous, or inconsistent with how the Account is titled. The trigger term \"specified\" carries the whole allocation of ownership rights.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c014", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c015", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The clause conclusively imputes an intent to create survivorship rights from the mere act of signing a signature card as co-owner (\"Each of you intends that ...\"), a deemed intention that may not match the depositors' actual intent or state ownership law. Whether signing alone establishes survivorship is the construction question.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.27}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c016", "issues": [
   {"issue": "\"All deposits are the property of all of the owners of the Account\" states an ownership rule as between the co-owners without qualification, though the same joint-account rules elsewhere treat the balance as belonging to the survivor and subject to set-off for one owner's debts. Whether this sentence governs beneficial ownership or only the Bank's dealings is unclear.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.17}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c017", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Within the same paragraph, \"None of you may instruct us to take away any of the rights of another\" sits against the following sentence permitting any single owner to give \"written notice ... not to pay any joint deposit\", after which the Bank may refuse all payments. The two sentences cannot both be applied to a one-owner freeze instruction.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.30}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c018", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c019", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The clause turns on \"any Authorized Signer\", a capitalized term that is never defined anywhere in the Agreement's Definitions or elsewhere. Who qualifies as an Authorized Signer, as opposed to an owner or a person merely listed on a signature card, is left open.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.19}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c020", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The clause provides for the Account to pass to the named beneficiary \"if that person is still living\" but says nothing about who takes if the beneficiary predeceases the trustee or if several beneficiaries are named. That gap, plus the deemed characterization as a \"Revocable Unwritten Trust\", is what would be construed.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.20}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c021", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c022", "issues": [
   {"issue": "This clause limits changes and withdrawals for \"either of these Trust Account types\" to what is \"permitted by the trust instrument or court order\", but the Unwritten Trust described in the immediately preceding clause exists precisely where no written trust instrument or court order was provided. Read together with that clause, the limitation has nothing to operate on, and it also contradicts the statement there that the trustee \"may withdraw all of the funds during your lifetime\".", "type": 2, "prob": 0.33}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c023", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c024", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c025", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The bracketed passage leaves the age of the \"minor\" to vary between eighteen and twenty-one \"depending on the jurisdiction ... and the circumstances\", and the transfer duty is triggered at majority \"or at another time determined by applicable law\". When the custodianship ends, and thus when the Bank must transfer the funds, is not fixed by the text.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.19}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c026", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The Bank's discretion to \"delay or refuse to give you provisional credit\" and its power to \"determine when final credit is received\" sit against Part III's Funds Availability Policy, which commits the Bank to fixed availability schedules for deposited items. Which controls when the Bank withholds provisional credit on an item that the Policy makes available is not resolved.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.25}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c027", "issues": [
   {"issue": "This clause states flatly that \"The Funds Availability Policy does not apply to items we have accepted for collection only\", but Part III's \"Non-U.S. Financial Institutions\" section, itself part of that Policy, prescribes availability for items sent for collection (generally within thirty Calendar Days, and by the third Business Day for Canadian items not sent for collection). The two cannot both be given effect for foreign collection items.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.27}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c028", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The clause manufactures a waiver of the customer's \"right to receive immediate notice of dishonor\" from the Bank's mere option to re-present the item (\"This means that you are waiving\"), without any separate assent or notice mechanism. Whether re-presentment effects that waiver is the construction question.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.19}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c029", "issues": [
   {"issue": "\"Typically, the Bank will cash checks drawn on other banks for its customers who have adequate available funds\" is hedged by \"typically\" and by the undefined standard of \"adequate available funds\", so it is unclear whether any obligation to cash is created at all. The word \"typically\" is what a court would have to construe.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c030", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c031", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c032", "issues": [
   {"issue": "This clause reserves a seven Calendar Day notice right for all Savings and Money Market Accounts, while the sub-account provision reserving the same seven-day right adds that \"the Bank does not presently exercise this right\". Whether the Bank may in fact impose the notice requirement on savings withdrawals turns on reading the two reservations together.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.19}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c033", "issues": [
   {"issue": "\"Repeated violations will result in the Account being closed or changed from a savings type Account to a transaction Account\" gives no number, period or standard for \"repeated\". The trigger for the mandatory consequence is therefore indeterminate.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c034", "issues": [
   {"issue": "This clause measures excess withdrawals \"in any calendar month\", whereas the immediately preceding savings-withdrawal clause measures them by \"any monthly period (based on your statement date)\". Which period governs a Holiday Club or Club Saver Account whose statement cycle is not a calendar month is unresolved.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.13}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c035", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The clause assures that sub-account transfers cause \"no adverse impact on the availability of the balances held in your Checking Account\", yet the same paragraph caps transfers out of the non-transaction sub-account at six per statement cycle and gives the Bank unfettered discretion over the transfer threshold. The assurance and the limitation pull against each other.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.21}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c036", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c037", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The clause reserves a seven Calendar Day notice right and then says \"the Bank does not presently exercise this right\", leaving open whether and with what notice the Bank could begin to exercise it. \"Does not presently exercise\" is the phrase requiring construction.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.15}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c038", "issues": [
   {"issue": "After five specific withholding triggers, item (f) adds \"there may be other reasons why we may be required to do so under applicable law\", a catch-all that swallows the enumerated list. Whether the list is exhaustive or merely illustrative is the classic construction question.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.14}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c039", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Reporting to consumer reporting agencies is triggered where an Account \"is closed for insufficient funds activity or other negative reason\", and \"other negative reason\" has no stated limit against the single specific it follows. The scope of the reportable closures is left open.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c040", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c041", "issues": [
   {"issue": "This clause excuses the Bank from sending a statement in three situations, yet the limitation-on-claims clause runs the customer's fourteen- and thirty-day reporting periods from the date \"we mailed a statement (or otherwise made it available to you)\". How the reporting deadlines operate when no statement is sent at all cannot be answered from either clause alone.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c042", "issues": [
   {"issue": "This clause states the review-and-report duty in open terms (\"promptly\", \"as soon as possible\", \"call us at once\"), while the later clause fixes fourteen Calendar Days as reasonable and thirty Calendar Days as an absolute bar. Whether the flexible standard here or the fixed periods there measure the customer's duty is a genuine question.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.21}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c043", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The clause deems statements and check images \"available\" as of the statement mailing date irrespective of receipt, which is the event that starts every reporting period. Whether \"available\" can be deemed to occur on mailing, particularly where the Bank never mails or the customer never receives, is what would be construed.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.24}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c044", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Failure or refusal to do \"these things\" — cooperate, seek criminal and civil penalties, file reports and complaints, and give a sworn statement — is deemed a ratification of the defect in the item. The scope of the required \"cooperation\" and the deemed ratification consequence are both open to construction.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.20}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c045", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The clause sets two different periods for the same conduct: fourteen Calendar Days is agreed to be \"a reasonable amount of time ... to review your Account statement and report any errors\", but claims are barred only if not reported within thirty Calendar Days. Which period governs a customer who reports on day twenty is not stated.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.34},
   {"issue": "The thirty Calendar Day bar covers \"any error, forgery or other problem relating to a matter shown on an Account statement\", while Part IV's Unauthorized Transfers section gives the customer sixty Calendar Days after the statement was mailed to report transfers he did not make. The two notice deadlines cannot both govern an unauthorized electronic debit appearing on a statement.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c046", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The Bank may destroy original checks \"not less than thirty (30) Calendar Days after the statement mailing date\", which is the same day the customer's thirty-Calendar-Day window to report a forgery under the preceding clause expires. Destruction of the evidence at the very moment the claim must be asserted is a tension between the two clauses.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c047", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c048", "issues": []},
 {"clause_id": "c049", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The Bank may refuse a check that is not \"in a form supplied by or previously approved by the Bank\" or which is \"incompletely or defectively drawn\", with no standard for approval and no definition of defective drawing. Both phrases leave the refusal right without an ascertainable boundary.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c050", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The definition of \"item\" closes a long enumerated list with \"any other instruction or order for the payment, transfer or withdrawal of funds\", a catch-all that governs which transactions are subject to the posting order and to overdraft fees. Whether the general words are limited by the enumerated specifics is the construction question.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.24}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c051", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The whole posting mechanism turns on \"your available Account balance\", a phrase never defined and used differently across subparagraphs (a) adds available deposits, (b) subtracts unpaid pending authorizations. Whether \"available balance\" means the ledger balance, the Funds Availability Policy balance, or that balance net of pending holds is exactly what a fee dispute would turn on.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.32}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c052", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The Bank deducts pending authorizations from the available balance \"subject to certain exceptions\", and the exceptions are then said to include an open-ended set of \"certain merchants that frequently request authorization for amounts in excess of the likely transaction amount\". Neither the exceptions nor the merchant category is fixed, so the customer cannot determine his available balance.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.34}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c053", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The category scheme leaves it uncertain which fees fall in category (ii), (iii) or (iv), and \"within categories i, ii, and iii, we post items in order from largest to smallest\" does not say whether items presented on the same day in different categories are ordered by category or by size. The high-to-low rule and the category boundaries are the terms in dispute.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.35}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c054", "issues": [
   {"issue": "Overdraft fees \"may be assessed on items presented for payment that bring your Account into a negative balance\", but \"negative balance\" is not defined and the same paragraph distinguishes the available balance (reduced by pending authorizations) from the balance on which fees are charged. Whether a fee is owed turns on which balance the phrase means and on the permissive \"may\".", "type": 1, "prob": 0.38}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c055", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The Bank promises \"advance notice\" before changing its processing order but fixes no period, form or method for that notice. What notice suffices, and whether it must satisfy the separate Amendments clause, is left open by the phrase \"advance notice\".", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c056", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The enumerated grounds for refusing an item end with \"(e) we believe is otherwise not properly payable\", a general catch-all sitting against the specific grounds that precede it. Whether \"otherwise not properly payable\" is confined to defects of the enumerated kind is the construction question.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.20}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c057", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The clause gives two examples of restrictive legends and then extends to legends \"and the like\", without any criterion for what other notations count as restrictive legends the Bank may disregard. The catch-all is the term needing construction.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.15}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c058", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The clause has the customer agree in advance never to postdate and denies him any remedy if an early-presented postdated check is paid, without any notice mechanism of the kind postdating rules ordinarily require. Whether that blanket advance waiver of \"any loss to you\" is effective is what would be construed.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.19}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c059", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The clause deems any debit initiated by a party to whom the customer voluntarily gave Account information \"authorized by you\", while Part IV's Unauthorized Transfers section and Electronic Check Conversions provisions give the customer rights over transfers made \"using information from your check\" without permission. The two treatments of the same debit cannot both stand.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.27}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c060", "issues": [
   {"issue": "An overdraft is defined here as an advance of funds greater than the amount \"that has become available in accordance with the Bank's Funds Availability Policy\", whereas the Processing Order clauses measure overdrafts against an \"available Account balance\" that is further reduced by pending debit-card authorizations. Which measure determines whether an overdraft — and hence a fee — has occurred cannot be answered from either provision alone.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.32}
 ]}
]}

